12 light in some other way? But the problem says each park will have 12, and there are 15 squares, so 15 x 12 is definitely 180. So total used is 180, total available is 200. 200 - 180 = 20. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe think in terms of fraction. Each of the 200 streetlights is used in 12 lights per square. But since there are 15 squares, total used would be 200 x (12/15). Let me calculate that. 200 divided by 15 is approximately 13.33, times 12 is... 13.33 x 12. Let me compute 12 x 13.33. 12 x 13 is 156, 12 x 0.33 is 3.96, so total is 156 + 3.96 = 159.96, so approximately 160. That's close to 180? Wait, wait, no. Wait, this seems conflicting. Wait, maybe my approach here is wrong.\n\nHold on, no. Wait, if each square has 12 streetlights, then total number of streetlights used is 15 squares x 12 streetlights = 180. But if we think in terms of the ratio, maybe that's different. Wait, perhaps I confused variables? Let me think again.\n\nIf there are 200 total streetlights, and they are allocated 15 squares, with each square using 12, then total used is 15 x 12. The unused is total minus that. So 200 - 180 = 20. So that's straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, if I messed up the multiplication, I could check. 15 x 12. Let's do it on paper:\n\n12 times 15 is 180. Yes, because 10x15=150, 2x15=30, total 180. So 180 used. So 200 - 180 = 20. So answer is 20 unused.\n\nI think that makes sense. I can't think of any other approach here. So I think the answer is 20.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we are given the following:\n\n- The New York City Council bought **200** streetlights.\n- There are **15 squares** in New York.\n- Each **square will get 12** new streetlights.\n\nThe goal is to find how many **unused** streetlights will remain.\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step Reasoning:\n\n1. **Calculate the total streetlights used in the squares:**\n   - Number of squares = 15\n   - Streetlights per square = 12  \n   $$ \\text{Total used streetlights} = 15 \\times 12 = 180 $$\n\n2. **Subtract the useful streetlights from the total to find the unused ones:**\n   - Available streetlights = 200  \n   $$ \\text{Unused streetlights} = 200 - 180 = \\boxed{20} $$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{20}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "The New York City Council bought 200 streetlights to modernize its squares. But they don't have enough squares to use all the streetlights bought. Knowing that there are 15 squares in New York and that each park will have 12 new streetlights bought by the city council, how many unused streetlights will remain?", "answer": "So the total number of streetlights that will be used is 15 squares * 12 streetlights/square = <<15*12=180>>180 streetlights\nThere will be 200 streetlights - 180 streetlights = <<200-180=20>>20 streetlights unused.\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: The New York City Council bought 200 streetlights to modernize its squares. But they don't have enough squares to use all the streetlights bought. Knowing that there are 15 squares in New York and that each park will have 12 new streetlights bought by the city council, how many unused streetlights will remain?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 576, 1532, 4261, 4311, 9074, 10788, 220, 17, 15, 15, 8592, 13826, 311, 6481, 551, 1181, 31340, 13, 1988, 807, 1513, 944, 614, 3322, 31340, 311, 990, 678, 279, 8592, 13826, 10788, 13, 57633, 429, 1052, 525], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 576, 3491, 2727, 279, 1532, 4261, 4311, 9074, 10788, 220, 17, 15, 15, 8592, 13826, 11, 714, 807, 646, 944, 990, 1105, 678, 369, 279, 220, 16, 20, 31340, 13, 8886, 6118, 686, 614, 220, 16, 17, 501, 8592, 13826, 13, 1205, 1