oxes.\n\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Adding the numbers: 12, 5, 4. 12 + 5 is 17, plus 4 is indeed 21. Then 33 minus 21 is 12. Yep, that seems right. So the answer should be 12 boxes.\n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. The reasoning is straightforward: add up the boxes sold already and subtract from the total required. The answer is 12.\n</think>\n\nKaylee needs to sell a total of 33 boxes of biscuits. She has already sold:\n\n- 12 boxes of **lemon biscuits**\n- 5 boxes of **chocolate biscuits**\n- 4 boxes of **oatmeal biscuits**\n\nAdding these amounts together gives the total number of boxes she has already sold:\n\n$$\n12 + 5 + 4 = 21\n$$\n\nSince she needs to sell **33 boxes**, the number of additional boxes she needs to sell is:\n\n$$\n33 - 21 = 12\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:** $\\boxed{12}$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Kaylee needs to sell 33 boxes of biscuits. So far, she has sold 12 boxes of lemon biscuits to her aunt, 5 boxes of chocolate biscuits to her mother, and 4 boxes of oatmeal biscuits to a neighbor. How many more boxes of biscuits does Kaylee need to sell?", "answer": "The number of boxes already sold is 12 + 5 + 4 = <<12+5+4=21>>21.\nKaylee needs to sell 33 − 21 = 12 more boxes.\n#### 12", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Kaylee needs to sell 33 boxes of biscuits. So far, she has sold 12 boxes of lemon biscuits to her aunt, 5 boxes of chocolate biscuits to her mother, and 4 boxes of oatmeal biscuits to a neighbor. How many more boxes of biscuits does Kaylee need to sell?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["12"], "solution": "12"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30145, 8501, 3880, 311, 4559, 220, 18, 18, 14697, 315, 85976, 13, 2055, 3041, 11, 1340, 702, 6088, 220, 1